The FCC prohibited it in the US.  The PDP's had too many emissions.
DEC didn't see the $$$ coming to support reengineering.
The J11 was late and problematic.

AT&T domestically picked up almost every used PDP11/70 in the states
they could grab from DEC in the 84-86 timeframe and had 'em refurbed.

DEC offered VAX for PDP11 swaps to pull the boxes from the field.
DEC pulled every internal 11/70 from Field Offices replacing them with 
11/74 pieces where possible.  (Try finding parts for never produced
an 11/70ish KB11-CM box 8-).)  I damn near freaked when the one 
in DEC Princeton looked like it was developing a bad backplane.

The problem was the company couldn't meet federal regs on the old boxes
without retooling.  They put the last of them in VAX 11/780 type corp
cabinets with the wire mesh over rubber gaskets to get the emissions
down.

I think that was OK for existing stuff but not for new production.

I hope someone from DEC engineering can answer this one with more 
information.

Bill

